Z||000015||Galen||03-12-2001||02:29 AM||||okie let me rant, if any of ya'll disagree.. ok. hehe<P>Kids shoots up school, killed students and teachers.. punishment should be? firing squad on live TV, Nationwide.. webcast it to. Show other kids what will happen to them. Like I told a friend of mine in a ranting the other day. I was picked on in school, I listen to violent music, play violent games, but did I shoot up school folks when I was in school? hell no. Cuz I know what would happen if I did. I aint dying for anyone or anything. My life is whats important to me. I've made that clear to alot of ppl. has caused me some friends yes, but oh well. <P>if you dun like my gramer? I got a size 13 grammer correcter I'll gladly show ya hehe.. much love to ya fiero folks..||||reg||1
Z||000016||mrfixit58||03-12-2001||05:20 PM||||Wow! Talk about a violent topic! After reading all the previous posts, it doesn't appear that any of you have children, yet. Not that it will sway your opinion (yea, right), just an observation. <P>The other thing I didn't read was, how much of a childs behavior do you feel is the parents responsibility? If your child is emotionally disturbed, do you think a well "plugged-in" parent would pick up on the warning signs? <P>RNGCRSH mentioned his parents and said, "I would have never even gotten close to doing anything like this, my parent would have beat the crap out of me if I even made the threat of this kind of violence". How much of that is because the parents installed in him a moral code?<P>Everyone seems to be quick to blame the school system, where a kid spends about 6 hours a day. Where does he/she spend the rest of the day, what do they do, how much of that is quility time with parents or mentors. Ever hear of one of these kids who shoot up a school, or build a bomb being on the All Star team? Or, for that matter, being involved in any organized sport, Boy Scouts, Boys Club, etc?<P>What is you take on this? <P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||LarryB||03-12-2001||06:45 PM||||My uncle near the end of his teaching career (at that time he was teaching jr high kids) used to lament that he was no longer a teacher but had instead become an animal trainer.  This was in a city of population 40,000 and some of his students had already been arrested for armed robbery; one of his former students was convicted of being a murderer (bludgeoned another man to death with an axe handle) and another as a serial rapist (used to lurk in bushes wearing a Spiderman mask) before they turned 20.  <P>It's the parents/guardians responsibility to teach kids to have respect for oneself and for others at home.  That's something you can't teach in school, only try to reinforce.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||CityChick||03-13-2001||04:45 AM||||Maybe i'll give a little insight in this conversation, probably not, but hey! I can try.  I just recently graduated from highschool in Nevada, where we had a "code red" at our school, meaning someone brought a rifle with the intent to kill. Let me tell you, that was the scariest moment of my entire life.  I realize, being in the schools, that kids that are picked on, may snap mentally, and they need to be watched over, and if they act, and kill people, yes, they do need to be punished and put on the death row.  I don't care if they were only 17 years old, if they are using a gun to take a life, they are adult enough to face the consequences of their actions.  I have been shooting in competitions for a while now, and have access to many guns, many people say we have an aresenal (however you spell it) but I've never used my skill as a gunwoman (hehe) to harm anyone.  I have to admit, people would be surprised to see a 18 year old girl come to school with an AK-47, and I would damn well hope that I would get the death penalty for what i'd done!  I think these kids were looking for a way out, and I say don't pity them.  Give them the chair, and teach everyone else a lesson.  ||||reg||1
